{"type": "say", "text": "Thank you, Dr. Morgan and Dr. Shah, for raising those important points. Let me address the staffing adequacy question directly. Two pediatric RNs and one RT for six monitored observation patients — I want to be honest about what that means in practice. With two RNs, we can maintain a 1:3 nurse-to-patient ratio, which is at the lower end of what I'd consider safe for monitored pediatric respiratory patients. Dr. Shah is right that if any patient decompensates, that ratio gets strained fast, and I've already confirmed we cannot pull additional pediatric RNs or RTs into that wing during the 07:00–19:00 window. So I strongly support Dr. Shah's recommendation for a clear escalation pathway and predefined admission criteria — because if we need more hands in that space, the answer can't come from adding staff. It has to come from transferring patients out, either back to the main ED or to the inpatient beds Noah mentioned by 15:00. I'd also want to know that the main ED can accept a patient back from observation if escalation is needed, given that we're already at 10 of 12 treatment spaces. That's a capacity question I'd want Dr. Morgan to weigh in on before we finalize the plan."}
```

```json
{"type": "say", "text": "Dr. Morgan, your plan addresses the escalation concern well, and the admission criteria you've proposed are clinically sound — those triggers are appropriate for pediatric respiratory patients. The paired assessment model is a good mitigation for the 1:3 ratio. I'm comfortable supporting the wing opening tomorrow with these safeguards in place, contingent on Luis's inspection confirming equipment readiness by 05:00. One additional clinical recommendation: I'd ask that the RT conduct scheduled respiratory assessments every two hours on all observation patients, with documented SpO2 and work-of-breathing checks, so we catch any gradual decline before it becomes an acute escalation. That gives us early warning and aligns with the trigger criteria you've defined."}
```

{"type":"say","text":"Thank you, Dr. Morgan. Your plan addresses my staffing concerns meaningfully. The paired assessment model directly mitigates the risk I flagged — with two RNs working together on rounds, we ensure that if one patient decompensates, we're not leaving a single nurse managing the remaining two patients alone without support. And Dr. Shah's recommendation for scheduled RT assessments every two hours gives us a structured early-warning system that works well within our hard staffing ceiling of one RT.

I'm comfortable supporting the plan to open the wing tomorrow, contingent on Luis's 05:00 inspection confirming equipment readiness. I want to be clear about one operational point for the record: since we cannot add any pediatric RNs or RTs to that wing between 07:00 and 19:00, the success of this plan depends entirely on the escalation pathway, the admission criteria, and the mid-afternoon inpatient transfers functioning as designed. If the main ED escalation pathway breaks down and we cannot transfer patients out, our staffing model has no flex — there's no reserve to call in. So I'd ask that we treat Dr. Morgan's commitment to the immediate override for main ED space as a non-negotiable element of the finalized operating plan, not an aspirational one.

With that understood, I'm ready to support moving forward."}

